{% extends "pages/_docs_guide.jinja" %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% from "data/docs.jinja" import docs_tested %}
{% set guide_slug = 'observe' %}
{% block guide_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="channels">
  <h2 id="channels">Choose a channel</h2>
  <p>
    Cuprum reports a run through several channels, and each answers a
    different question. Pick by the question, and keep every callback
    bounded:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lifecycle events.</strong> {{ api.ref('observe') }} delivers an {{ api.ref('ExecEvent') }} as a command plans, starts, writes, exits, or times out, and one <code>settled</code> event with its terminal outcome. Correlate by <code>exec_id</code>, not <code>pid</code>, which the operating system recycles.</li>
    <li><strong>Lines.</strong> <code>on_line</code> or {{ api.ref('SafeCmd.lines') }}, in <a href="/cuprum/docs/guides/output/">Control output</a>, for progress as it happens.</li>
    <li><strong>A heartbeat.</strong> <code>idle_after</code> says that no monitored output arrived; it says nothing about whether the child is stuck.</li>
    <li><strong>Control operations.</strong> {{ api.ref('observe_control') }} reports what <code>signal()</code>, <code>terminate()</code>, or <code>kill()</code> on a {{ api.ref('RunningCommand') }} did to the child, which the exit code alone cannot say.</li>
    <li><strong>Echo fallbacks.</strong> {{ api.ref('observe_echo') }} reports a truncated line, or a sink that could not encode or had closed.</li>
    <li><strong>Stream operations.</strong> One aggregate event per completed drain or pipeline transfer, for throughput.</li>
    <li><strong>The Rust pump.</strong> Whether each pipeline hop used the native pump or fell back to Python; a decline is a routing decision, not a failure. See the <a href="/cuprum/internals/rust-extension/">Rust extension</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Only {{ api.ref('observe') }} hooks can fail a run. Every other channel
    is fail-open: a hook that raises is logged at <code>WARNING</code> and
    the operation it was observing carries on. Their events carry only
    bounded values, never output, argument vectors, or exception text, so the
    ready-made metrics hooks can label series without unbounded cardinality.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="settled">
  <h2 id="settled">Read the one terminal outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Every execution whose <code>plan</code> event began observation ends with
    exactly one <code>settled</code> event, including a spawn failure, a
    timeout, and a cancellation, which have no <code>exit</code> event to
    speak of. Its {{ api.ref('cuprum.events', 'TerminalOutcome') }} is one of
    <code>exit_zero</code>, <code>exit_nonzero</code>, <code>timeout</code>,
    <code>cancelled</code>, or <code>error</code>; it carries a
    <code>pid</code> and <code>exit_code</code> only when those are known and
    never any exception text. Phases will be added again, so give a hook that
    matches on <code>phase</code> a default arm:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('settled.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import ExecEvent, Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="phases")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
outcomes = []


def hook(event: ExecEvent) -> None:
    match event.phase:
        case "settled":
            outcomes.append(event.terminal_outcome)
        case _:
            pass  # Phases this hook does not track, including future ones.


with sh.observe(hook):
    python("-c", "pass").run_sync()
assert [outcome.value for outcome in outcomes] == ["exit_zero"]
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    Every event also carries the execution's <code>env_mode</code> and
    <code>capture_mode</code>, known before the child spawns and describing
    the whole run, so a byte-exact run under a replacement environment is
    distinguishable from a text run under an overlay in every log record,
    span, and counter. A binding adds <code>resolved_path</code>, the
    executable that ran, to the event and the result. Catalogue lookup and
    allowlist refusals happen before an <code>exec_id</code> is minted and
    emit no events at all.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="telemetry">
  <h2 id="telemetry">Log, measure, and trace runs</h2>
  <p>
    The adapters turn lifecycle events into structured log records
    ({{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.logging_adapter', 'logging_adapter') }}),
    metrics ({{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.metrics_adapter', 'metrics_adapter') }}),
    and trace spans ({{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.tracing_adapter', 'tracing_adapter') }}).
    Each is an observe hook. <code>InMemoryMetrics</code> and
    <code>InMemoryTracer</code> are reference backends; production code
    implements the <code>MetricsCollector</code> protocol, or the
    <code>Tracer</code> and <code>Span</code> protocols, over its own
    telemetry library.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('telemetry.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import logging
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh
from cuprum.adapters.logging_adapter import structured_logging_hook
from cuprum.adapters.metrics_adapter import InMemoryMetrics, MetricsHook
from cuprum.adapters.tracing_adapter import InMemoryTracer, TracingHook

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="telemetry")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
metrics = InMemoryMetrics()
tracer = InMemoryTracer()
logger = logging.getLogger("myapp.telemetry")

with (
    sh.observe(structured_logging_hook(logger=logger)),
    sh.observe(MetricsHook(metrics)),
    sh.observe(TracingHook(tracer)),
):
    python("-c", "print('traced')").run_sync()

assert metrics.counters["cuprum_executions_total"] == 1
assert [span.name for span in tracer.spans] and tracer.spans[0].ended
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    <code>MetricsHook</code> counts <code>cuprum_failures_total</code> and
    observes <code>cuprum_duration_seconds</code> from <code>settled</code>,
    so the failure counter covers every outcome except <code>exit_zero</code>,
    and <code>cuprum_terminal_outcomes_total</code> is labelled by the
    outcome. <code>cuprum_executions_total</code> and
    <code>cuprum_failures_total</code> carry <code>env_mode</code> and
    <code>capture_mode</code> labels, bounded by construction;
    <code>resolved_path</code> deliberately never becomes a label.
    <code>TracingHook</code> closes its span on <code>settled</code>. The
    structured logging adapter and the tracing adapter record
    <code>argv</code> in full, so a secret passed as an argument reaches the
    log unless a <a href="#redaction">redaction policy</a> masks it. Never
    turn untrusted arguments into metric labels.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="redaction">
  <h2 id="redaction">Keep secrets out of telemetry</h2>
  <p>
    The adapters export what a run looked like, and that can include
    secrets: structured logging and tracing record the full argument vector,
    and events can carry environment values, output lines, notes, and tag
    values. Passing a secret in a file, rather than as an argument or
    environment value, keeps it out of these records. Where that is not
    possible, a {{ api.ref('cuprum.redaction', 'RedactionPolicy') }} masks
    the text it is told about before it is exported:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('telemetry_redaction.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import logging
import re
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh
from cuprum.adapters.logging_adapter import structured_logging_hook
from cuprum.adapters.tracing_adapter import InMemoryTracer, tracing_hook
from cuprum.redaction import RedactionPolicy, redacting_hook

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="redaction")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
policy = RedactionPolicy(
    literals=("token-value",),
    patterns=(re.compile(r"session-[0-9]+"),),
)
tracer = InMemoryTracer()
exports = []
logger = logging.getLogger("myapp.telemetry")
with (
    sh.observe(structured_logging_hook(logger=logger, redaction=policy)),
    sh.observe(tracing_hook(tracer, redaction=policy)),
    sh.observe(redacting_hook(exports.append, policy)),
):
    result = python(
        "-c", "import sys; print(sys.argv[1])", "token-value session-314"
    ).run_sync()

assert result.stdout == "token-value session-314\n"
assert exports and tracer.spans
assert "token-value" not in repr(exports) + repr(tracer.spans)
assert "session-314" not in repr(exports) + repr(tracer.spans)
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It masks only what it is given.</strong> Rules are literal strings (<code>literals=</code>) and compiled regular expressions (<code>patterns=</code>); the policy does not guess at secrets. Each match becomes the mask, <code>[REDACTED]</code> by default or the <code>mask=</code> text, and overlapping matches become a single mask.</li>
    <li><strong>It is opt-in at each destination.</strong> Pass <code>redaction=policy</code> to <code>structured_logging_hook()</code>, <code>tracing_hook()</code> or <code>TracingHook</code>, {{ api.ref('logging_hook') }}, and <code>GitHubActionsSink</code>, and wrap any other observer as <code>redacting_hook(observer, policy)</code>. A destination without a policy exports text unchanged, so configure every destination that leaves the process.</li>
    <li><strong>It changes exports, not the run.</strong> The command's arguments and environment, the captured result, and events seen by other observers are untouched.</li>
    <li><strong>It covers free text.</strong> Argument items, environment values, output lines, notes, and top-level string tag values are redacted. The program name, project, paths, tag keys, and numeric and enum fields are not.</li>
    <li><strong>It fails closed.</strong> If a rule fails, <code>RedactionError</code> is raised and nothing is exported in raw form. Like any observe-hook failure, it fails the run.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Metrics need no policy: their labels never carry arguments, environment
    values, output, or tags. The <code>GitHubActionsSink</code> accepts the
    same <code>redaction=</code> argument for its group and annotation
    titles; the <code>group</code> and <code>annotate_failure</code> flags
    have no redaction setting, and redaction never alters the mirrored child
    output.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="control">
  <h2 id="control">Watch what a stop request did</h2>
  <p>
    An exit code of <code>-15</code> is the same whether the child took the
    first <code>SIGTERM</code> or needed <code>SIGKILL</code> after its grace
    ran out, and a stop refused because the child had already gone leaves no
    trace at all. {{ api.ref('observe_control') }} registers a
    {{ api.ref('ControlHook') }} on a context-local channel; every completed
    <code>signal()</code>, <code>terminate()</code>, and <code>kill()</code>
    on a {{ api.ref('RunningCommand') }} then reports one
    {{ api.ref('ControlEvent') }} with the closed
    {{ api.ref('ControlOperation') }} and {{ api.ref('ControlOutcome') }},
    the measured duration, and the run's <code>exec_id</code>:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('control_observation.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import asyncio
import sys

from cuprum import ExecutionContext, Program, ProgramCatalogue, observe_control, sh

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="control")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
command = python("-c", "import time; time.sleep(30)")
events = []


async def stop_a_child() -> None:
    async with await command.start(
        context=ExecutionContext(cancel_grace=0.2),
    ) as handle:
        await handle.terminate()


with observe_control(events.append):
    asyncio.run(stop_a_child())
assert [(event.operation, event.outcome) for event in events] == [
    ("terminate", "delivered"),
]
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    An outcome of <code>escalated</code> means the child ignored
    <code>SIGTERM</code> and had to be killed; <code>refused_exited</code> and
    <code>refused_unsupported</code> mirror the two <code>signal()</code>
    refusals. Emission is fail-open, so an observer that raises cannot
    replace the refusal the caller is owed or interrupt the cleanup under
    way, and a caller who registers nothing pays no clock read.
    {{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.control_metrics', 'control_metrics_hook') }}
    projects the events to bounded counters and a duration histogram whose
    only labels are the operation and the outcome; the PID reaches the event
    and the span but never a label, because a recycled PID would fold two
    children into one series.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="streams">
  <h2 id="streams">Measure stream throughput</h2>
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('cuprum.stream_observation', 'observe_stream_operation') }}
    registers a hook for the pure-Python stream paths. It receives one event
    per completed drain or transfer, never one per read, with the bytes
    moved, the number of reads, and the duration.
    {{ api.ref('cuprum.adapters.stream_metrics', 'stream_operation_metrics_hook') }}
    turns those into counters and a histogram whose only labels are the
    closed <code>operation</code> and <code>outcome</code> values. Observer
    failures are logged and suppressed, so observing never changes a run.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('stream_metrics.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, sh
from cuprum.adapters.metrics_adapter import InMemoryMetrics
from cuprum.adapters.stream_metrics import stream_operation_metrics_hook
from cuprum.stream_observation import observe_stream_operation

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="stream-metrics")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
metrics = InMemoryMetrics()
with observe_stream_operation(stream_operation_metrics_hook(metrics)):
    python("-c", "print('hello')").run_sync()
assert metrics.counters["cuprum_stream_operation_bytes_total"] > 0
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="actions">
  <h2 id="actions">Present output in GitHub Actions</h2>
  <p>
    On GitHub Actions, Cuprum can frame a run's echoed output in a
    collapsible log group and turn a failed run into an
    <code>::error::</code> annotation. Both are presentation only: capture,
    exit codes, and the result are unchanged. The framing activates only when
    the parent process runs on GitHub Actions (<code>GITHUB_ACTIONS</code> is
    <code>true</code>); elsewhere a run behaves as if it were absent.
  </p>
  <h3>Group and annotate flags</h3>
  <p>
    <code>group</code> and <code>annotate_failure</code> on
    {{ api.ref('RunOutputOptions') }} are the shorthand for the common case.
    They build a <code>GitHubActionsSink</code> and store it as the options'
    sink, so shared options can carry them safely:
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('ci_flags.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import dataclasses
import io
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, sh
from cuprum.sinks import GitHubActionsSink

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="ci-flags")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)

shared = RunOutputOptions(echo=True, group=True, annotate_failure=True)
assert isinstance(shared.sink, GitHubActionsSink)
result = python("-c", "print('building')").run_sync(output=shared)
assert result.ok and result.stdout == "building\n"

# An explicit sink wins, so shared flagged options never displace it.
explicit = GitHubActionsSink(io.StringIO(), force=True)
assert dataclasses.replace(shared, sink=explicit).sink is explicit

try:
    RunOutputOptions(group="yes")
except ValueError:
    pass
else:
    raise AssertionError("a non-bool flag should be rejected")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  <p>
    The flags are independent, and both default to <code>False</code>.
    <code>group=True</code> frames the run in a <code>::group::</code> and
    <code>::endgroup::</code> pair and protects echoed output with a
    stop-commands lease, so child output cannot inject workflow commands; a
    pipeline receives one group for the whole run.
    <code>annotate_failure=True</code> writes one <code>::error::</code>
    annotation when the run ends in a non-zero exit, a timeout, or an error;
    on its own it frames no group and takes no lease.
  </p>
  <p>
    Workflow commands go to the parent's stderr. An explicit
    <code>sink=</code> takes precedence and makes both flags no-ops, which is
    what makes them safe in shared options; pass it on a new
    <code>RunOutputOptions</code> or through
    <code>dataclasses.replace(shared, sink=...)</code>. The flags carry no
    <code>force</code>, and anything but a <code>bool</code> raises
    <code>ValueError</code>. Run grouped commands one after another when they
    share the parent's stderr, or their frames can interleave.
  </p>
  <h3>Configure the sink directly</h3>
  <p>
    Use {{ api.ref('cuprum.sinks', 'GitHubActionsSink') }} for anything the
    flags do not cover: a custom <code>title</code>, another
    <code>destination</code>, <code>force=True</code> to reproduce the
    framing locally, or <code>emit_group=False</code> and
    <code>emit_annotation=False</code> to switch off either half of the
    frame.
  </p>
{% call ui.code_panel('actions_sink.py', 'Tested', foot=[docs_tested]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import io
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, sh
from cuprum.sinks import GitHubActionsSink

catalogue = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(sys.executable, name="ci")
python = sh.make(Program(sys.executable), catalogue=catalogue)
workflow_log = io.StringIO()
sink = GitHubActionsSink(workflow_log, title="Build", force=True)
result = python("-c", "print('building')").run_sync(
    output=RunOutputOptions(echo=True, sink=sink),
)
assert result.ok and result.stdout == "building\n"
assert workflow_log.getvalue().startswith("::group::Build")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
</section>
{% endblock %}
